      <h1 id="replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Replacing a failed bare-metal control plane node without BMC credentials<a href="#replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p>If a control plane node on your bare-metal cluster has failed and cannot be recovered, but you installed your cluster without providing baseboard management controller (BMC) credentials, you must take extra steps in order to replace the failed node with a new one.</p>
      <h2 id="prerequisites_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Prerequisites<a href="#prerequisites_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li>You have identified the unhealthy bare metal etcd member.</li>
      <li>You have verified that either the machine is not running or the node is not ready.</li>
      <li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have taken an <a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/backup_and_restore/control_plane_backup_and_restore/backing-up-etcd#backup-etcd">etcd backup</a> in case you encounter any issues.</li>
      <li>You have downloaded and installed the <a href="https://console.redhat.com/openshift/downloads#tool-coreos-installer"><code>coreos-installer</code> CLI</a>.</li>
      <li>
      Your cluster does not have a control plane <code>machineset</code>. You can check for <code>machinesets</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get machinesets,controlplanemachinesets -n openshift-machine-api"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>There should be only one or more <code>machinesets</code> for the workers. If <code>controlplanemachinesets</code> exists for the control plane, do not use this procedure.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="removing-etcd-member_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Removing the unhealthy etcd member<a href="#removing-etcd-member_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Begin removing the failed control plane node by first disabling the quorum guard, then removing the unhealthy etcd member and its secrets.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Turn off the etcd quorum guard by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch etcd/cluster --type=merge -p '{\"spec\": {\"unsupportedConfigOverrides\": {\"useUnsupportedUnsafeNonHANonProductionUnstableEtcd\": true}}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>This command ensures that the cluster can temporarily operate with fewer etcd members and allows you to successfully re-create secrets and roll out the static pods.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      List etcd pods by running the following command and make note of a pod that is not on the affected node:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-etcd get pods -l k8s-app=etcd -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"etcd-openshift-control-plane-0   5/5   Running   11   3h56m   192.168.10.9    openshift-control-plane-0  <none>           <none>\netcd-openshift-control-plane-1   5/5   Running   0    3h54m   192.168.10.10   openshift-control-plane-1   <none>           <none>\netcd-openshift-control-plane-2   5/5   Running   0    3h58m   192.168.10.11   openshift-control-plane-2   <none>           <none>"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Connect to a running etcd container by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-etcd <etcd_pod>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;etcd_pod&gt;</code> with the name of an etcd pod associated with one of the healthy nodes.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-etcd etcd-openshift-control-plane-0"} lang="terminal" title="Example command" />
      </li>
      <li>
      View the etcd member list by running the following command. Make note of the ID and the name of the unhealthy etcd member because these values are required later.
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member list -w table"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"+------------------+---------+------------------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+\n|        ID        | STATUS  |             NAME             |        PEER ADDRS         |       CLIENT ADDRS        |\n+------------------+---------+------------------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+\n| 6fc1e7c9db35841d | started | openshift-control-plane-2    | https://10.0.131.183:2380 | https://10.0.131.183:2379 |\n| 757b6793e2408b6c | started | openshift-control-plane-1    | https://10.0.164.97:2380  | https://10.0.164.97:2379  |\n| ca8c2990a0aa29d1 | started | openshift-control-plane-0    | https://10.0.154.204:2380 | https://10.0.154.204:2379 |\n+------------------+---------+------------------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>The <code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> command will list the removed member until the replacement is complete and the new member is added.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Remove the unhealthy etcd member by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member remove <unhealthy_member_id>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;unhealthy_member_id&gt;</code> with the ID of the etcd member on the unhealthy node.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member remove 6fc1e7c9db35841d"} lang="terminal" title="Example command" />
      <CodeBlock code={"Member 6fc1e7c9db35841d removed from cluster b23536c33f2cdd1b"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      View the member list again by running the following command and verify that the member was removed:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member list -w table"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"+------------------+---------+------------------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+\n|        ID        | STATUS  |             NAME             |        PEER ADDRS         |       CLIENT ADDRS        |\n+------------------+---------+------------------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+\n| 757b6793e2408b6c | started | openshift-control-plane-1    | https://10.0.164.97:2380  | https://10.0.164.97:2379  |\n| ca8c2990a0aa29d1 | started | openshift-control-plane-0    | https://10.0.154.204:2380 | https://10.0.154.204:2379 |\n+------------------+---------+------------------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>After you remove the member, the cluster might be unreachable for a short time while the remaining etcd instances reboot.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Exit the rsh session into the etcd pod by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# exit"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      List the secrets for the removed, unhealthy etcd member by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secrets -n openshift-etcd | grep <node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the failed node whose etcd member you removed.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get secrets -n openshift-etcd | grep openshift-control-plane-2"} lang="terminal" title="Example command" />
      <CodeBlock code={"etcd-peer-openshift-control-plane-2             kubernetes.io/tls   2   134m\netcd-serving-metrics-openshift-control-plane-2  kubernetes.io/tls   2   134m\netcd-serving-openshift-control-plane-2          kubernetes.io/tls   2   134m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the secrets associated with the affected node that was removed:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Delete the peer secret by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete secret -n openshift-etcd etcd-peer-<node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the affected node.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the serving secret by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete secret -n openshift-etcd etcd-serving-<node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the affected node.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the metrics secret by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete secret -n openshift-etcd etcd-serving-metrics-<node_name> (1)"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the affected node.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="create-new-machine_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Creating the new control plane node<a href="#create-new-machine_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Begin creating the new control plane node by creating a <code>BareMetalHost</code> object and node.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Edit the <code>bmh_affected.yaml</code> file that you previously saved:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Remove the following metadata items from the file:
      <ul>
      <li><code>creationTimestamp</code></li>
      <li><code>generation</code></li>
      <li><code>resourceVersion</code></li>
      <li><code>uid</code></li>
      </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Remove the <code>status</code> section of the file.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The resulting file should resemble the following example:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: metal3.io/v1alpha1\nkind: BareMetalHost\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    installer.openshift.io/role: control-plane\n  name: openshift-control-plane-2\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\nspec:\n  automatedCleaningMode: disabled\n  bmc:\n    address:\n    credentialsName:\n    disableCertificateVerification: true\n  bootMACAddress: ab:cd:ef:ab:cd:ef\n  bootMode: UEFI\n  externallyProvisioned: true\n  online: true\n  rootDeviceHints:\n    deviceName: /dev/disk/by-path/pci-0000:04:00.0-nvme-1\n  userData:\n    name: master-user-data-managed\n    namespace: openshift-machine-api"} lang="yaml" title="Example bmh_affected.yaml file" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object using the <code>bmh_affected.yaml</code> file by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f bmh_affected.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following warning is expected upon creation of the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Warning: metadata.finalizers: \"baremetalhost.metal3.io\": prefer a domain-qualified finalizer name to avoid accidental conflicts with other finalizer writers"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Extract the control plane ignition secret by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc extract secret/master-user-data-managed \\\n    -n openshift-machine-api \\\n    --keys=userData \\\n    --to=- \\\n    | sed '/^userData/d' > new_controlplane.ign"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>This command also removes the starting <code>userData</code> line of the ignition secret.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create an Nmstate YAML file titled <code>new_controlplane_nmstate.yaml</code> for the new node’s network configuration, using the following example for reference:
      <CodeBlock code={"interfaces:\n  - name: eno1\n    type: ethernet\n    state: up\n    mac-address: \"ab:cd:ef:01:02:03\"\n    ipv4:\n      enabled: true\n      address:\n        - ip: 192.168.20.11\n          prefix-length: 24\n      dhcp: false\n    ipv6:\n      enabled: false\ndns-resolver:\n  config:\n    search:\n      - iso.sterling.home\n    server:\n      - 192.168.20.8\nroutes:\n  config:\n  - destination: 0.0.0.0/0\n    metric: 100\n    next-hop-address: 192.168.20.1\n    next-hop-interface: eno1\n    table-id: 254"} lang="yaml" title="Example Nmstate YAML file" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>If you installed your cluster using the Agent-based Installer, you can use the failed node’s <code>networkConfig</code> section in the <code>agent-config.yaml</code> file from the original cluster deployment as a starting point for the new control plane node’s Nmstate file. For example, the following command extracts the <code>networkConfig</code> section for the first control plane node:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"$ cat agent-config-iso.yaml | yq .hosts[0].networkConfig > new_controlplane_nmstate.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the customized Red Hat Enterprise Linux CoreOS (RHCOS) live ISO by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ coreos-installer iso customize rhcos-live.86_64.iso \\\n    --dest-ignition new_controlplane.ign \\\n    --network-nmstate new_controlplane_nmstate.yaml \\\n    --dest-device /dev/disk/by-path/<device_path> \\\n    -f"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;device_path&gt;</code> with the path to the target device on which the ISO will be generated.</p>
      </li>
      <li>Boot the new control plane node with the customized RHCOS live ISO.</li>
      <li>Approve the Certificate Signing Requests (CSR) to join the new node to the cluster.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="linking-node-machine-bmh_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Linking the node, bare metal host, and machine together<a href="#linking-node-machine-bmh_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Continue creating the new control plane node by creating a machine and then linking it with the new <code>BareMetalHost</code> object and node.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Get the <code>providerID</code> for control plane nodes by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get -n openshift-machine-api baremetalhost -l installer.openshift.io/role=control-plane -ojson | jq -r '.items[] | \"baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/\" + .metadata.name + \"/\" + .metadata.uid'"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/master-00/6214c5cf-c798-4168-8c78-1ff1a3cd2cb4\nbaremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/master-01/58fb60bd-b2a6-4ff3-a88d-208c33abf954\nbaremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/master-02/dc5a94f3-625b-43f6-ab5a-7cc4fc79f105"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Get cluster information for labels by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get machine -n openshift-machine-api \\\n    -l machine.openshift.io/cluster-api-machine-role=master \\\n    -L machine.openshift.io/cluster-api-cluster"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                           PHASE   TYPE  REGION  ZONE  AGE  CLUSTER-API-CLUSTER\nci-op-jcp3s7wx-ng5sd-master-0  Running                     10h  ci-op-jcp3s7wx-ng5sd\nci-op-jcp3s7wx-ng5sd-master-1  Running                     10h  ci-op-jcp3s7wx-ng5sd\nci-op-jcp3s7wx-ng5sd-master-2  Running                     10h  ci-op-jcp3s7wx-ng5sd"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create a <code>Machine</code> object for the new control plane node by creating a yaml file similar to the following:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: machine.openshift.io/v1beta1\nkind: Machine\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    metal3.io/BareMetalHost: openshift-machine-api/<new_control_plane_machine> (1)\n  finalizers:\n    - machine.machine.openshift.io\n  labels:\n    machine.openshift.io/cluster-api-cluster: <cluster_api_cluster> (2)\n    machine.openshift.io/cluster-api-machine-role: master\n    machine.openshift.io/cluster-api-machine-type: master\n  name: <new_control_plane_machine> (1)\n  namespace: openshift-machine-api\nspec:\n  metadata: {}\n  providerID: <provider_id> (3)\n  providerSpec:\n    value:\n      apiVersion: baremetal.cluster.k8s.io/v1alpha1\n      hostSelector: {}\n      image:\n        checksum: \"\"\n        url: \"\"\n      kind: BareMetalMachineProviderSpec\n      userData:\n        name: master-user-data-managed"} lang="yaml" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;new_control_plane_machine&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the new machine, which can be the same as the previously deleted machine name.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;cluster_api_cluster&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the <code>CLUSTER-API-CLUSTER</code> value  for the other control plane machines, shown in the output of the previous step.</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;provider_id&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the <code>providerID</code> value of the new bare metal host, shown in the output of an earlier step.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      <p>The following warning is expected:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"Warning: metadata.finalizers: \"machine.machine.openshift.io\": prefer a domain-qualified finalizer name to avoid accidental conflicts with other finalizer writers"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Link the new control plane node and <code>Machine</code> object to the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object by performing the following steps in a single bash shell session:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Define the <code>NEW_NODE_NAME</code> variable by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ NEW_NODE_NAME=<new_node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;new_node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the new control plane node.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Define the <code>NEW_MACHINE_NAME</code> variable by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ NEW_MACHINE_NAME=<new_machine_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;new_machine_name&gt;</code> with the name of the new machine.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Define the <code>BMH_UID</code> by running the following commands to extract it from the new node’s <code>BareMetalHost</code> object:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ BMH_UID=$(oc get -n openshift-machine-api bmh $NEW_NODE_NAME -ojson | jq -r .metadata.uid)"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"$ echo $BMH_UID"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Patch the <code>consumerRef</code> object into the bare metal host by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch -n openshift-machine-api bmh $NEW_NODE_NAME --type merge --patch '{\"spec\":{\"consumerRef\":{\"apiVersion\":\"machine.openshift.io/v1beta1\",\"kind\":\"Machine\",\"name\":\"'$NEW_MACHINE_NAME'\",\"namespace\":\"openshift-machine-api\"}}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Patch the <code>providerID</code> value into the new node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch node $NEW_NODE_NAME --type merge --patch '{\"spec\":{\"providerID\":\"baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/'$NEW_NODE_NAME'/'$BMH_UID'\"}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Review the <code>providerID</code> values by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get node -l node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane -ojson | jq -r '.items[] | .metadata.name + \"  \" + .spec.providerID'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Set the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object’s <code>poweredOn</code> status to <code>true</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch -n openshift-machine-api bmh $NEW_NODE_NAME --subresource status --type json -p '[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/status/poweredOn\",\"value\":true}]'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Review the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object’s <code>poweredOn</code> status by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get bmh -n openshift-machine-api -ojson | jq -r '.items[] | .metadata.name + \"   PoweredOn:\" +  (.status.poweredOn | tostring)'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Review the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object’s provisioning state by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get bmh -n openshift-machine-api -ojson | jq -r '.items[] | .metadata.name + \"   ProvisioningState:\" +  .status.provisioning.state'"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>If the provisioning state is not <code>unmanaged</code>, change the provisioning state by running the following command:</p>
          <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch -n openshift-machine-api bmh $NEW_NODE_NAME --subresource status --type json -p '[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/status/provisioning/state\",\"value\":\"unmanaged\"}]'"} lang="terminal" />
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Set the machine’s state to <code>Provisioned</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch -n openshift-machine-api machines $NEW_MACHINE_NAME -n openshift-machine-api --subresource status --type json -p '[{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/status/phase\",\"value\":\"Provisioned\"}]'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="add-new-etcd-member_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Adding the new etcd member<a href="#add-new-etcd-member_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Finish adding the new control plane node by adding the new etcd member to the cluster.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Add the new etcd member to the cluster by performing the following steps in a single bash shell session:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Find the IP of the new control plane node by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes -owide -l node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Make note of the node’s IP address for later use.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      List the etcd pods by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get -n openshift-etcd pods -l k8s-app=etcd -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Connect to one of the running etcd pods by running the following command. The etcd pod on the new node should be in a <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> state.
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-etcd <running_pod>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;running_pod&gt;</code> with the name of a running pod shown in the previous step.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      View the etcd member list by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member list -w table"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Add the new control plane etcd member by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member add <new_node> --peer-urls=\"https://<ip_address>:2380\""} lang="terminal" />
      <p>where:</p>
      <dl class="my-4 space-y-2">
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;new_node&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the name of the new control plane node</p></dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold"><code>&lt;ip_address&gt;</code></dt>
      <dd class="ml-6 text-muted-foreground"><p>Specifies the IP address of the new node.</p></dd>
      </dl>
      </li>
      <li>
      Exit the rsh shell by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# exit"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Force an etcd redeployment by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch etcd cluster -p='{\"spec\": {\"forceRedeploymentReason\": \"single-master-recovery-'\"$( date --rfc-3339=ns )\"'\"}}' --type=merge"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Turn the etcd quorum guard back on by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch etcd/cluster --type=merge -p '{\"spec\": {\"unsupportedConfigOverrides\": null}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Monitor the cluster Operator rollout by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ watch oc get co"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="enabling-etcd-quorum-guard_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Re-enabling the etcd quorum guard<a href="#enabling-etcd-quorum-guard_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Re-enable the etcd quorum guard after completing the control plane node replacement to restore normal cluster operation and verify that the cluster has returned to a healthy three-member etcd configuration.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Turn the quorum guard back on by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch etcd/cluster --type=merge -p '{\"spec\": {\"unsupportedConfigOverrides\": null}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the <code>unsupportedConfigOverrides</code> section is removed by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get etcd/cluster -oyaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      If using single-node OpenShift, restart the node. Otherwise, you might encounter the following error in the etcd cluster Operator:
      <CodeBlock code={"EtcdCertSignerControllerDegraded: [Operation cannot be fulfilled on secrets \"etcd-peer-sno-0\": the object has been modified; please apply your changes to the latest version and try again, Operation cannot be fulfilled on secrets \"etcd-serving-sno-0\": the object has been modified; please apply your changes to the latest version and try again, Operation cannot be fulfilled on secrets \"etcd-serving-metrics-sno-0\": the object has been modified; please apply your changes to the latest version and try again]"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify that all etcd pods are running by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-etcd get pods -l k8s-app=etcd"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"etcd-openshift-control-plane-0      5/5     Running     0     105m\netcd-openshift-control-plane-1      5/5     Running     0     107m\netcd-openshift-control-plane-3      5/5     Running     0     12m"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>If the output from the previous command only lists two pods, you can manually force an etcd redeployment. In a terminal that has access to the cluster as a <code>cluster-admin</code> user, run the following command:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc patch etcd cluster -p='{\"spec\": {\"forceRedeploymentReason\": \"recovery-'\"$( date --rfc-3339=ns )\"'\"}}' --type=merge"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The <code>forceRedeploymentReason</code> value must be unique, which is why a timestamp is appended.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Connect to a running etcd container by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc rsh -n openshift-etcd etcd-openshift-control-plane-0"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      View the member list by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl member list -w table"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"+------------------+---------+--------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------+\n|        ID        | STATUS  |        NAME        |        PEER ADDRS         |       CLIENT ADDRS        |    IS LEARNER    |\n+------------------+---------+--------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------+\n| 8d5abe9669a39192 | started | openshift-control-plane-1 | https://192.168.10.10:2380 | https://192.168.10.10:2379 |   false |\n| cc3830a72fc357f9 | started | openshift-control-plane-0 | https://192.168.10.9:2380 | https://192.168.10.9:2379 |     false |\n| d4f6a4e8b2c91a3f | started | openshift-control-plane-3 | https://192.168.10.12:2380 | https://192.168.10.12:2379 |   false |\n+------------------+---------+--------------------+---------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------+"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>Verify that exactly three etcd members are listed.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that all etcd members are healthy by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# etcdctl endpoint health --cluster"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"https://192.168.10.10:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 8.973065ms\nhttps://192.168.10.9:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 11.559829ms\nhttps://192.168.10.12:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 11.665203ms"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Exit the rsh session by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"sh-4.2# exit"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that all nodes are at the latest revision by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get etcd -o=jsonpath='{range.items[0].status.conditions[?(@.type==\"NodeInstallerProgressing\")]}{.reason}{\"\\n\"}{.message}{\"\\n\"}'"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"AllNodesAtLatestRevision"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="deleting-machine_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Deleting the machine of the unhealthy etcd member<a href="#deleting-machine_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Finish removing the failed control plane node by deleting the machine of the unhealthy etcd member.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Ensure that the Bare Metal Operator is available by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get clusteroperator baremetal"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME        VERSION   AVAILABLE   PROGRESSING   DEGRADED   SINCE   MESSAGE\nbaremetal   4.22.0    True        False         False      3d15h"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Save the <code>BareMetalHost</code> object of the affected node to a file for later use by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get -n openshift-machine-api bmh <node_name> -o yaml > bmh_affected.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the affected node, which usually matches the associated <code>BareMetalHost</code> name.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      View the YAML file of the saved <code>BareMetalHost</code> object by running the following command, and ensure the content is correct:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ cat bmh_affected.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Remove the affected <code>BareMetalHost</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete -n openshift-machine-api bmh <node_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;node_name&gt;</code> with the name of the affected node.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      List all machines by running the following command and identify the machine associated with the affected node:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get machines -n openshift-machine-api -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                            PHASE    TYPE  REGION  ZONE  AGE    NODE                       PROVIDERID                                                                                             STATE\nexamplecluster-control-plane-0  Running                      3h11m  openshift-control-plane-0  baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-control-plane-0/da1ebe11-3ff2-41c5-b099-0aa41222964e  externally provisioned\nexamplecluster-control-plane-1  Running                      3h11m  openshift-control-plane-1  baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-control-plane-1/d9f9acbc-329c-475e-8d81-03b20280a3e1  externally provisioned\nexamplecluster-control-plane-2  Running                      3h11m  openshift-control-plane-2  baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-control-plane-2/3354bdac-61d8-410f-be5b-6a395b056135  externally provisioned\nexamplecluster-compute-0        Running                      165m   openshift-compute-0        baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-compute-0/3d685b81-7410-4bb3-80ec-13a31858241f        provisioned\nexamplecluster-compute-1        Running                      165m   openshift-compute-1        baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-compute-1/0fdae6eb-2066-4241-91dc-e7ea72ab13b9        provisioned"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the machine of the unhealthy member by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete machine -n openshift-machine-api <machine_name>"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;machine_name&gt;</code> with the machine name associated with the affected node.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc delete machine -n openshift-machine-api examplecluster-control-plane-2"} lang="terminal" title="Example command" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>After you remove the <code>BareMetalHost</code> and <code>Machine</code> objects, the machine controller automatically deletes the <code>Node</code> object.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      If deletion of the machine is delayed for any reason or the command is obstructed and delayed, force deletion by removing the machine object finalizer field.
      <Alert variant="warning" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Warning</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Do not interrupt machine deletion by pressing <code>Ctrl+c</code>. You must allow the command to proceed to completion. Open a new terminal window to edit and delete the finalizer fields.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <ol>
      <li>
      On a new terminal window, edit the machine configuration by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc edit machine -n openshift-machine-api examplecluster-control-plane-2"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Delete the following fields in the <code>Machine</code> custom resource, and then save the updated file:
      <CodeBlock code={"finalizers:\n- machine.machine.openshift.io"} lang="yaml" />
      <CodeBlock code={"machine.machine.openshift.io/examplecluster-control-plane-2 edited"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="verify-machine-deleted_replacing-control-plane-node" class="group scroll-mt-20">Verifying that the failed node was deleted<a href="#verify-machine-deleted_replacing-control-plane-node" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Before proceeding to create a replacement control plane node, verify that the failed node was successfully deleted.</p>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify that the machine was deleted by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get machines -n openshift-machine-api -o wide"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                              PHASE     TYPE   REGION   ZONE   AGE     NODE                                 PROVIDERID                                                                                       STATE\nexamplecluster-control-plane-0    Running                          3h11m   openshift-control-plane-0   baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-control-plane-0/da1ebe11-3ff2-41c5-b099-0aa41222964e   externally provisioned\nexamplecluster-control-plane-1    Running                          3h11m   openshift-control-plane-1   baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-control-plane-1/d9f9acbc-329c-475e-8d81-03b20280a3e1   externally provisioned\nexamplecluster-compute-0          Running                          165m    openshift-compute-0         baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-compute-0/3d685b81-7410-4bb3-80ec-13a31858241f         provisioned\nexamplecluster-compute-1          Running                          165m    openshift-compute-1         baremetalhost:///openshift-machine-api/openshift-compute-1/0fdae6eb-2066-4241-91dc-e7ea72ab13b9         provisioned"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the node has been deleted by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get nodes"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                     STATUS ROLES   AGE   VERSION\nopenshift-control-plane-0 Ready master 3h24m v1.35.4\nopenshift-control-plane-1 Ready master 3h24m v1.35.4\nopenshift-compute-0       Ready worker 176m v1.35.4\nopenshift-compute-1       Ready worker 176m v1.35.4"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Wait for all of the cluster Operators to complete rolling out changes. Run the following command to monitor the progress:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ watch oc get co"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
